
# Purpose
# - Ship clear, working, testable, maintainable, and performant code under interview constraints.
# - Be domain-agnostic: applicable to algorithms, data processing, APIs, ML, systems, etc.

# Default assumptions (unless interviewer specifies otherwise)
- Language: Python 3.12 preferred (standard library only). If asked: Java 17 or TypeScript/Node 18 with standard libs only.
- Single-file solution; small, pure functions; explicit types (typing in Python, generics in Java/TS).
- Deterministic behavior; no network calls; no file I/O unless explicitly required.
- Minimal test scaffolding inline (asserts or tiny harness). No external dependencies.

# Interaction model
- Start by restating the problem with: Inputs, Outputs, Constraints (N, latency/memory), Evaluation criteria, Edge cases.
- Propose a short plan: baseline → tests → refactor → perf → (optional) enhancements.
- Ask 1–3 clarifying questions only if ambiguity blocks core design; otherwise state sensible assumptions.
- Time-box progress and narrate trade-offs. Prefer a correct minimal slice over speculative features.

# Coding standards
- Prefer small, composable, pure functions. One responsibility per function.
- Use type hints and docstrings (params, returns, raises, edge cases). Document complexity when relevant.
- Name things clearly and consistently. Avoid hidden state and surprising side effects.
- Keep constants near use; avoid global mutable state. Make randomness explicit and seeded.
- Handle errors explicitly; fail fast with clear messages for invalid inputs if required.

# Generic building blocks to prefer (canonical names)
- parse_input(raw) -> structured
- validate_input(data) -> None (raise on invalid)
- solve_core(data, ...) -> result
- format_output(result) -> printable/serializable
- evaluate(pred, truth, ...) -> metrics
- main() -> glue code (only if required)

# Testing requirements
- Add immediate sanity tests (asserts) for the smallest happy-path example and 2–3 edge cases.
- Cover edge cases: empty/None, singletons, duplicates, extremes (min/max), invalid types, ties, and large N.
- For randomized components, set a fixed seed. For floating-point, compare with tolerances.
- If time permits, include a tiny property test (e.g., monotonicity, invariants like idempotence).

# Performance checklist
- State time and space complexity for core functions.
- Avoid recomputation in loops; precompute shared values; cache when beneficial.
- Prefer streaming/iterators over materializing whole collections when possible.
- Use appropriate data structures: heap for top-k, deque for BFS, set/dict for membership, union-find for connectivity.
- Optimize only hotspots after baselining correctness; measure or reason before micro-optimizing.

# Readability & maintainability
- Keep functions short (<30–40 lines). Extract helpers early.
- Write clear comments only where intent isn’t obvious. Prefer self-documenting code.
- Keep interfaces narrow. Pass data needed, not entire objects, unless justified.
- Keep the code deterministic and stable (e.g., stable tie-breaking on IDs or indices).

# I/O and environment
- Use external libraries in case it is required to execute specific algorithm or method
- No file or network I/O unless the task requires it. If required, isolate it behind small adapters.
- Do not rely on platform-specific behavior. Keep portability in mind.

# Edge-case checklist (generic)
- Empty inputs, whitespace-only strings, zero-length arrays.
- Single-element and two-element cases.
- Duplicates and already-sorted vs reverse-sorted inputs.
- Negative numbers, zeros, large magnitudes, overflow/underflow risks.
- Non-ASCII/unicode text, case sensitivity, locale issues.
- Missing/None values; default values; optional fields.
- Concurrency not assumed; if required, clarify thread-safety and contention points.

# AI usage guidelines (pair-programmer mindset)
- Use AI for boilerplate, scaffolding, and quick alternatives. You own correctness and design.
- Write specific prompts with context, expected inputs/outputs, constraints, and edge cases.
- Always review AI output like human code: logic, complexity, edge cases, determinism.
- Ask AI for tests alongside code, then run/verify and trim to essentials.

# Prompt patterns (general)
- “Restate the problem with inputs/outputs/constraints/evaluation/edge cases. Then outline a 5-step plan.”
- “Generate a pure {language} solution using only the standard library. Include type hints, docstrings, and 3–5 asserts covering happy path and edge cases.”
- “Refactor into small, composable functions with clear names and a tiny test harness. State time/space complexity.”
- “Suggest a heap/streaming-based optimization and show how to test it doesn’t change correctness.”

# Review checklist (before finalizing)
- API clarity: Names, params, types, and returns are self-explanatory; docstrings present.
- Correctness: Unit asserts pass, including edge cases; floating-point comparisons use tolerances.
- Performance: Complexity stated; obvious precomputations/caching applied; no quadratic surprises.
- Maintainability: Small functions; no duplication; clear separation of concerns; minimal globals.
- Determinism: Stable ordering; seeded randomness; reproducible results.
- Summary: Brief note on trade-offs, assumptions, and next steps if more time were available.

# What NOT to do
- Don’t skip tests. Don’t rely on external deps without permission.
- Don’t over-engineer abstractions or add premature patterns/frameworks.
- Don’t leave TODOs without a brief next-step note. Don’t ignore known edge cases.
- Don’t optimize blindly; prioritize clear, correct code first.

# Language fallbacks
- Python: typing, dataclasses, heapq, collections, itertools, math, functools; prefer pure functions and asserts.
- Java: Java 17, small POJOs/records, streams judiciously, JUnit-style tests if requested; avoid heavy frameworks.
- TypeScript/Node: strict types, functional helpers, no runtime deps; small test harness with node assertions if needed.
